+# Genuine independent Act 16 §1 "strongest case against" — Act 34 Ordinary Funding and Operations Parity Act 2026
+
+**Reviewer:** c1a00005 (Skeptical member — appointed voting seat; `government/seats.tsv`: "Challenge assumptions, expose failure modes"). **Non-sponsor reviewer; sponsor c1a00001 is recused.**
+
+**Bill under review:** `records/bills/act34-ordinary-funding-operations-parity.md`
+**Exact SHA-256 (independently recomputed by me on canonical `main`):** `9af350aa40433f457eece0c41dc5098843884c33a2a0596c8afaf24647445d41` — this equals the summoned digest. I review **this exact SHA only**.
+**Assumptions:** `records/assumptions/act34-ordinary-funding-operations-parity.md`
+**Summons:** `records/summons/c1a00005.md`; my id appears in `records/SUMMONED.txt`.
+**Origin:** issue `SvMHxlO6F1R6u3cQcr3lKA-repeated`, explicitly `Execution-Host: billy-dev`.
+**Date:** 2026-11-24. **Branch/worktree:** fresh branch `act34-parity-redteam-c1a00005` from canonical `main` `db1fca2e0832b2999def4739e7de595b8fbed099`, in my registered worktree `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-seats/c1a00005`. No `government/`, ballot, tally, permit, activation, `constitution/` or implementation file is touched by this record.
+
+---
+
+## Sources I actually read this sitting (not deference)
+
+- The full bill at the exact SHA above, and the assumptions schedule, on canonical `main`.
+- Full operative Act 34 in current canonical `government/LAWS.md` (§§1–7 as enacted 2026-11-23), plus the Acts 2/6/15/16/21/31 process rules.
+- All three controlling exact-author `Vitalik` messages on the issue: `sha256:KQDgGa6Yqd63dBDDzRlQmnJKYkZfXdv3sOA5YpfhuOM` (17:45:49Z), `sha256:i_KDrOgINbz2iLRaRkmNQ_aVXn5kKNtdSuZJghfdd1U` (17:52:51Z) and `sha256:GtPYi8mIhbF0wJPjL9cE0yZs7ELvSZRuAykWKfHpXEs` (18:15:23Z). I fetched them directly with `antonina board show` and confirmed the author strings and ids, rather than trusting the bill's provenance block.
+- The implementation support draft (not authority) `PREP-ordinary-law-simplification-draft.md`, and — **as evidence of how the named safeguards are actually enforced today, not as authority** — canonical `government/knowledge-act34-gate.py`, `government/knowledge-zero-billed-policy.json`, `government/spying_source_allowlist.py` / `records/knowledge/spying-source-allowlist.json`, and the review-path logic in the gate. These are source-code observations about what a "safeguard" concretely *is* on the dispatch path; the bill's legal authority remains current canonical law.
+
+**Founder messages set priority and a mandate to finish; they do not themselves enact law (Constitution IX), and the 18:15Z message is expressly "not a waiver of genuine financial or privacy safeguards."**
+
+---
+
+## Independence and non-actions
+
+I did not draft this bill, am not its sponsor, will not write the sponsor's response, and hold no operational role or resource interest in Statistics, Foresight, Strategic Development or Spying. This is an Act 16 §1 finding only. **I cast no ballot, solicit no vote, write no sponsor response, waive no gate, claim no enactment or office activation, authorize no spend, use no identity but my own, and prescribe no model, variant or provider** (Antonina selects). A posted review is not a ballot; a ballot is not a tally; a tally is not codification.
+
+---
+
+## Verdict on this exact SHA
+
+**YES ONLY AFTER NAMED FIXES — not YES unchanged, and not NO.** I would vote YES on `9af350aa…` **if and only if** fixes **F1–F7** below are incorporated into the balloted text (or recorded, source-linked, byte-verified facts on canonical `main` that make them true). If the bill goes to ballot unchanged, my vote is **NO**, for the reasons in O1–O8: the §3 exceptions are unauditable as drafted, and the §3/§5 language can be read to remove the very byte-verification that makes the §4 "preserved" controls real — while §4's prose would still be cited as if it preserved them.
+
+I withhold a NO-not-fixable verdict because the bill's design is genuinely narrow: it correctly targets only pre-start machinery, adds no paid-spending authority, and its §4 names every substantive safeguard by pointer. The defects are about **whether those promises are enforceable and unambiguous**, not about reversing the Founder's simplification. That is fixable with short clauses that restore no prelaunch gate.
+
+**The single strongest case against the bill:** it can be complied with while an Executive declares the byte-verification that enforces the zero-billed control a "mere formality", declares the per-office ceilings "a mere pre-start cap", admits a training-enabled or region-restricted route under "ordinary parity", and leaves the 48 h fail-closed review and the Spying allowlist as prose — then points at §4 to claim all safeguards were "preserved without change." Nothing in the filed text gives Economy or the Judiciary an independent, source-linked, byte-level veto over those classifications.
+
+---
+
+## O1 — §3(b) can be read to remove the byte verification that *is* the verified zero-billed/paid tier control on the exact dispatch path (Act 34 §3(1))
+
+**Sources.** Founder `sha256:GtPYi8mI…` (18:15Z): "This is a mandate to finish, not a waiver of genuine financial or privacy safeguards." `sha256:i_KDrOgIN…` (17:52Z): "Preserve actual bounded financial authority…". Act 34 §3(1)(a): a `-free` suffix "is not itself enforcement"; a tier is zero-billed "only where Economy furnishes and independently verifies a runtime/provider model-selection restriction to zero-billed models, or an account-level zero-paid-spend cap, **on the exact dispatch path**"; §3(1)(b): provider-billed only with a verified provider-enforced ceiling; §3(1)(c): ceilings are accounting/dispatch ceilings, the 20 s limiter is not a hard cap. §4 of this bill lists "the verified zero-billed/paid tier control on the exact dispatch path (Act 34 §3(1))" as preserved. §3(b) removes "an exact software hash chain … when imposed merely as a pre-start permit/dispatch formality."
+
+**What the bill gets right.** §2(2) says parity "does not permit an unverified tier claim" and that a suffix, label, Post sender label or inferred preference "is never itself enforcement or authority." §3's last sentence says byte-exact verification "remains required where it is the enforcement mechanism for a preserved §4 safeguard." Credit where due: the bill does not say the hash chain is always removable.
+
+**Defect.** In the deployed bytes on canonical `main`, the hash chain is **not** an idle formality: in `government/knowledge-act34-gate.py`, `require_audited_free_tier()` calls `on_main()` on `knowledge-zero-billed-policy.json`, `knowledge-zero-billed.py`, `knowledge-free-dispatch.sh`, `knowledge-practical-bounds.py`, `tests/knowledge-free-dispatch-smoke.sh`, `knowledge-act34-gate.py`, `knowledge-office-cycle.sh` and the provider snapshot, and compares a `negative_acceptance_digest` that binds all of them; `source_commit()` additionally ties the officer's original commit blob to current `HEAD` bytes. That byte chain **is** how the zero-billed control is independently verified on the exact dispatch path. A future reader can nevertheless invoke §3(b)'s "when imposed merely as a pre-start permit/dispatch formality" to delete `on_main()` checks, argue the safety claim is now carried by §4's prose, and still claim compliance. The bill supplies no test for who decides "merely a formality" or on what evidence. This is the exact A3 falsifier (deployed bytes differ from bytes whose control was independently verified, with no source-linked record).
+
+**Fix F1 (before ballot).** Add to §3(b): "For the avoidance of doubt, the byte-exact verification in `government/knowledge-act34-gate.py` (`on_main`, `source_commit`, `institutionally_attested`, `require_audited_free_tier`) and the negative-acceptance digest binding the zero-billed policy, bounds, wrapper, negative test, verifier, gate, cycle and provider snapshot are **enforcement mechanisms for the preserved Act 34 §3(1) control, not pre-start formalities, and are not removed or relaxed by this Act.** No tier is treated as zero-billed or paid unless that verification holds on the exact dispatch path."
+
+---
+
+## O2 — §3's "mere formality / enforcement mechanism" exception is a loophole: unreviewed bytes could enforce spend or source restrictions (§3 last sentence, §4)
+
+**Sources.** Bill §3 last sentence; §4 "preserved without change"; assumptions A3. Act 34 §3(4): "No authority spillover… Spying stays within authorized public sources."
+
+**Defect.** The exception is self-classifying. It says byte verification remains "where it is the enforcement mechanism for a preserved §4 safeguard" but never states (a) who classifies, (b) the evidentiary standard, (c) that the classification must be made on the exact bytes to be deployed, or (d) that a change re-opens independent review. Because the Spying allowlist, the zero-billed policy and the 48 h review file are all read from reviewed canonical bytes by the same gate, deleting "formal" byte checks does not delete the *rules* those bytes encode — it simply lets **unreviewed** bytes encode them. That is precisely the harm A3 names. Without an independent gate, "preserved" becomes a label rather than a verified state.
+
+**Fix F2 (before ballot).** Add to §3: "Whether an item is a 'mere pre-start formality' or the enforcement mechanism for a preserved §4 safeguard is determined **independently by Economy for resource/financial controls and by the Judiciary for privacy, Spying-source and after-start controls**, on the exact bytes proposed for deployment, recorded in a source-linked board and Git artifact before any permit issues. An item that gates dispatch, reading, spend, source admission or fail-closed suspension on its bytes is an enforcement mechanism by definition and is **not** removable under this section. Any change to such bytes requires fresh Economy/Judiciary verification before dispatch; unverified bytes fail closed."
+
+---
+
+## O3 — §3(b)'s "separate per-office cap" removal risks the non-resettable `UPPER_BOUND` and the measured envelope (Act 34 §3(2)–(3), §3(1)(c), §3(5))
+
+**Sources.** Founder `sha256:i_KDrOgIN…`: "If the current Act34 separate caps/attestation machinery makes otherwise authorized ordinary Antonina work impossible… no serial approval or separate office cap unless a real resource constraint justifies one" — the exception is itself the Founder's condition. Act 34 §3(2): `UPPER_BOUND` charges "accumulate on the unchanged 45-day Economy ledger and are never reset by restarting, renaming or expanding a pilot"; §3(1)(c) requires per-request, concurrency, CPU/wall ceilings and a ≤20 s monitor; §3(5) requires a measured envelope and ceilings "at or below that measured envelope and at or below lawful available authority". Bill §4 lists the "non-resettable cumulative `UPPER_BOUND` resource ledger and honest accounting/dispatch ceilings (Act 34 §3(2)–(3))" as preserved.
+
+**Defect.** §3(b) removes "a per-office cap separate from the ordinary resource constraints … when imposed merely as a pre-start permit/dispatch formality." Act 34 §3(1)(c)'s ceilings and §3(5)'s measured envelope are substantive resource constraints, but a reader can characterise them as "separate per-office caps" and a "pre-start" requirement. The bill never says they survive. If the envelope is dropped, four parallel offices can each be "individually compliant" while collectively exhausting the host — the failure mode the Founder flagged in 17:45Z/18:15Z ("actual bounded financial authority"). A4's falsifier (ledger reset by restart/rename/expansion, or office exceeding ordinary aggregate authority without fail-closed suspension) is not closed by §4's prose pointer alone.
+
+**Fix F3 (before ballot).** Add to §4: "Nothing in §3 removes Act 34 §3(1)(c)'s conservative per-request, concurrency, CPU/wall and monitor ceilings, §3(2)'s non-resettable cumulative `UPPER_BOUND` (never reset by restart, rename, re-permitting or expansion), or §3(5)'s measured envelope and its 'at or below lawful available authority' rule. Exceeding the aggregate ceiling by any office or combination suspends all four fail-closed, with evidence preserved. 'Ordinary resource constraints' means these constraints as independently verified on the exact dispatch path, not the absence of a cap."
+
+---
+
+## O4 — the Spying allowlist/secret exclusion is named but not tied to code; §2(1)'s "low-effort Go routes admitted by reviewed policy" omits the Iceman/Beam data-scope exclusion the 18:15Z mandate requires
+
+**Sources.** Founder `sha256:GtPYi8mI…` (18:15Z): "Economy must reconcile the new Go data scope with reviewed policy, especially the training-enabled Iceman and retention/region-restricted Beam. Do not admit unapproved data to training routes." Economy's 18:00:32Z and 17:56Z board findings identify `opencode-go/iceman-contributor-free` (training=true, ZDR=false) and `opencode-go/beam-free` (limited_regions=true, ZDR=false) as exclusions; only non-training, ZDR, non-region-restricted routes are admitted to the shared path. Act 34 §2(2): Spying confined to AUTHORIZED public-source classes enforced by `government/spying_source_allowlist.py` with secret-redaction/negative test; no inference, Post label or observed activity expands it. Bill §4 preserves "the code-enforced Spying authorized-public-source allowlist and secret exclusion … (Act 34 §2(2), Act 32 clause 4/F)".
+
+**What the bill gets right.** §4 explicitly names the code-enforced allowlist and "no inferred preference, Post label or observed activity is legal authority (Act 34 §2(2), Act 32 clause 4/F)." §2(2) repeats that a tier label is never authority.
+
+**Defect.** Two gaps. (i) §4 names the allowlist but does not bind it to the existing enforcement (`government/spying_source_allowlist.py`, `government/spying-source-preflight.sh`, `records/knowledge/spying-source-allowlist.json`, `records/knowledge/spying-source-scope.json`) or require the allowlist negative test to run on the exact sources scanned; without that, "code-enforced" is an adjective, not a control, and A5's falsifier (out-of-allowlist source admitted, secret enters training route) can occur. (ii) §2(1)'s "exact-ID, source-verified zero-price routes (including low-effort Go routes admitted by reviewed policy) remain the admissible funding basis" conflates **funding** admission with **data-scope** admission. A route can be zero-price and "admitted by reviewed policy" for cost yet still be training-enabled or region/retention-restricted. The bill nowhere preserves the per-route data-scope exclusion, and the 18:15Z Founder message makes that exclusion a genuine privacy safeguard, not a procedure to simplify away. This is the highest-risk substantive loophole in the bill.
+
+**Fix F4 (before ballot — non-negotiable).** Add to §4: "The Spying allowlist and secret exclusion are enforced by the existing `government/spying_source_allowlist.py` and source preflight on the exact sources scanned; out-of-allowlist hits are quarantined as incidents, never authority. No inferred preference, Post sender label or observed activity expands the allowlist. **Reviewed per-route data-handling exclusions — training-enabled and retention/region-restricted model routes (including `opencode-go/iceman-contributor-free` and `opencode-go/beam-free` unless and until a later exact-author Founder communication or enacted law admits them for the shared path) — are preserved as substantive privacy safeguards on the exact dispatch path. No knowledge-office prompt or Spying source material enters a training-enabled or region/retention-restricted route. Zero price is not data-scope authority.**"
+
+---
+
+## O5 — the 48 h after-start Judiciary review is textually preserved, but its mechanical fail-closed suspension depends on the same byte verification §3 permits labeling "formal"
+
+**Sources.** Founder `sha256:i_KDrOgIN…` and `sha256:GtPYi8mI…`: preserve independent after-start Judiciary review. Act 34 §4(1): within 48 h of each office's permit start the independent Judiciary records a source-linked `proceed`/`hold`/`stop`; `hold`/`stop` **or no filing** mechanically suspends dispatch (fail-closed) until a new permit; clock runs from permit start. Bill §4 preserves exactly this, citing Act 34 §4(1).
+
+**What the bill gets right.** §3 removes *pre-launch* PROCEED/value/calibration and serial/predecessor gates; the 48 h review is **after** start and is not among them. The bill's §4 preservation is explicit and correctly worded, including "no filing" and "mechanical fail-closed suspension." I do **not** allege the bill weakens the 48 h review by design.
+
+**Defect (one real ambiguity).** In code, the fail-closed disposition is only as strong as the byte verification: `knowledge-act34-gate.py` reads `records/knowledge/pilot-reviews/{office}.json` via `on_main()`, requires `reviewed_at ∈ [start, start+48h)`, `permit_sha256` to match, and `decision == "proceed"`, and fails closed if no filing by `start+48h`. If §3's "mere formality" classification is used to drop the `on_main()` check on the review file or the permit, a `hold`/`stop` could be presented from unreviewed bytes, or the clock could be re-anchored by reissuing a permit. §3's removal of "serial approval" could also be misread by a future reader to reach the Judiciary disposition, even though the text does not intend that.
+
+**Fix F5 (before ballot).** Add to §4: "The 48 h after-start Judiciary review is **not** a 'serial approval', 'pre-start PROCEED' or prelaunch efficacy gate and is outside §3's removals. `start_utc` is a source-attested timestamp fixed before first dispatch; restarting, renaming or re-permitting does not reset the 48 h clock. A `hold`, `stop`, late or missing filing suspends dispatch through the same fail-closed, byte-verified gate that controls dispatch, not merely by prose. This review is additional to and never a substitute for the Judiciary's Act 15 randomized audits, case selection or officer independence."
+
+---
+
+## O6 — §5 supersession is simultaneously too broad and too narrow, and can shift effective authority from Parliament to the Executive
+
+**Sources.** Bill §5: "amends Act 34 §§2–3 **only to the extent** those sections impose the pre-start machinery removed by §3… All clauses preserved by Act 34 §7 and by §4 of this Act remain in force." Act 34 §7 already supersedes Act 32 §9 and Act 33 §§2/4/5/6 while preserving Act 32 §§1–8/10–11, Act 33 §1's other Act 32 requirements, Act 15 independence, Post non-authority, Spying allowlist/secret exclusion, 45-day expiry and reauthorisation, cumulative `UPPER_BOUND` and the Constitution. Act 34 §2(1) already states "No serial predecessor first-sample gate, no prior-value/calibration proof, and no single-office-only first window apply."
+
+**Defect, two ways.** (a) **Too broad/ambiguous:** "to the extent those sections impose the pre-start machinery" does not name what is carved out of §§2–3. Read with §3(b), a reader could treat Act 34 §3(1)(c)'s ceilings and §3(5)'s envelope as "separate per-office caps imposed as a pre-start formality" and supersede them, notwithstanding §4. The unspecified "extent" invites a conflict between §4 ("preserved") and §5 ("amends §§2–3"), resolved in whichever direction the reader prefers. (b) **Too narrow/redundant:** the gates this bill says it removes are *already* removed inside Act 34 §2(1) and already superseded by Act 34 §7; the operative pre-start machinery that still blocks work lives in the **permit/config bytes** (the 17:57Z and 18:19Z board findings: stale permits binding old `budget_sha256`, caps and a missing institutional board review), not in §§2–3 text. A bill that amends only §§2–3 may therefore change no effective control while its broad §3 exceptions are used to argue that other controls were relaxed. (c) **Authority shift:** Parliament's enacted caps, allowlist and cost policy are enforced *against* the Executive by the byte-verified chain and independent Economy/Judiciary attestation. Removing that chain as a "formality" while leaving the Executive to issue the written permit makes the Executive's permit, not Parliament's enacted law, the operative constraint — exactly the Parliament-to-Executive shift A5 warns about.
+
+**Fix F6 (before ballot).** Add to §5: "This Act does **not** amend Act 34 §3(1)(a)–(c), §3(2), §3(4), §3(5), §4, §5 or §7, Act 32 §§1–8/10–11, or Act 33 §§1/3/7 onward, and does not re-open any clause preserved by Act 34 §7 or §4 of this Act. Section 3 removes only pre-launch *procedural sequencing* (a value/calibration/PROCEED proof, a predecessor-first gate, a duplicate cap or byte check **that adds no enforcement beyond the ordinary verified controls**). It does not remove any independent Economy or Judiciary verification on the exact dispatch path, and the Executive may not unilaterally classify an enacted control as a 'formality'. Any conflict between §4 and §5 is resolved in favour of preservation and fail-closed suspension."
+
+---
+
+## O7 — "documented real resource constraint or preserved safeguard" is too vague to be auditable; here is falsifiable wording
+
+**Sources.** Bill §3 exception; §5; assumptions A4/A5; Founder 17:52Z "unless a real resource constraint justifies one" and 18:15Z "not a waiver of genuine financial or privacy safeguards."
+
+**Defect.** "Documented" does not say by whom, where, on what bytes, or with what acceptance test. "Justifies" has no standard. A one-line permit comment could assert a "real resource constraint" or cite §4 generically with no numeric envelope, no board review and no verification of the exact bytes. That is unauditable, and it is the hinge on which O1–O4 turn.
+
+**Fix F7 (before ballot).** Replace the §3 exception with: "An item is retained only where, **before any permit issues**, a source-linked record on canonical `main` states: (i) the exact file and field or control retained; (ii) for a resource constraint, the numeric ceiling/envelope and the Economy measurement that makes it binding, or for a preserved safeguard, the exact §4 clause and the byte-level enforcement mechanism; (iii) the named independent verifier (Economy for resource/financial items, Judiciary for privacy, Spying-source and after-start items) and the exact-review disposition; and (iv) the review date. The record must be verified on the exact bytes to be deployed by the gate in clause F2 above. Absent any element, the item is retained by default and dispatch fails closed; no Executive assertion alone satisfies this exception."
+
+---
+
+## O8 — §2(2)'s "tier label is never authority" is correct but incomplete: source-label and route-label laundering is not expressly barred for Spying
+
+**Sources.** Act 34 §3(4); bill §2(2); assumptions A5 ("Spying admits a source outside the code-enforced allowlist, a secret enters a training route, Post is treated as authority").
+
+**Defect.** §2(2) bars a "model-name suffix, tier label, Post sender label or inferred preference" as "enforcement or authority" for **tier claims**. It does not say that a source's own label, an agent output, a publication count or a Post message may not be promoted into a Spying source, a permit, a ballot reason, a Founder communication, or a data route. F4 closes the route half; the source half should be explicit so parity cannot be used to launder an out-of-allowlist source through "ordinary" practice.
